\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Self-consistency---majority voting over multiple samples---yields large gains on discrete-answer reasoning \citep{wang2023selfconsistency}, and test-time compute scaling has emerged as a complementary axis to model scaling \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys}.
But named entity recognition (NER) poses a structurally different challenge: the output is a \textit{set} of entities, two samples may extract overlapping but non-identical sets, and there is no obvious majority to vote on.

Prior work on confidence estimation for LLM-based IE has conflated \textit{quality estimation} (which inputs does the model handle reliably?) with \textit{sample selection} (which of $N$ outputs is best?).
Existing self-consistency methods \cite{wang2023selfconsistency, chen2024usc, taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty} assume outputs can be grouped by equivalence---an assumption that breaks for entity sets with partial overlap.
\citet{yao2026consistency} examine consistency--accuracy correlations for LLM-based IE using exact-match agreement; we extend their work by introducing structural matching (Soft Jaccard, a variant using per-type max cardinality; \S\ref{sec:signals}) that captures partial overlap, separating quality estimation from best-of-$N$ selection, and evaluating across a three-dataset difficulty gradient with a free-form decoding ablation.

Our central finding is the \textbf{correlation-selection gap} (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}): consistency signals achieve moderate-to-strong quality estimation ($\rho$ up to 0.45, substantially exceeding random baselines) but fail at sample selection despite substantial oracle headroom.
The gap has two causes: instance-level signals (4 of 5) assign identical QE scores to all candidates; consensus-proximity (MBR) converts these to per-sample rankings, but utility correlates with F1 across instances, not within them (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
LP---the only natively per-sample signal---is too compressed within instances to discriminate (median range 0.041 nats; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_overview}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap. \textbf{Left}: high inter-sample agreement correlates with high F1 (quality estimation). \textbf{Right}: instance-level signals assign identical QE scores; MBR converts these to per-sample rankings but does not improve selection. LP (natively per-sample) provides distinct scores but is too compressed to discriminate (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We evaluate five signals from three families---structural, surface, and model-internal---across three NER benchmarks (SciERC \cite{luan2018scierc}, CoNLL-2003 \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll}, WNUT-17 \cite{derczynski2017wnut}), using two 8B models (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B) with a 4B ablation (Qwen3-4B).
We include one RE benchmark (SciERC) as an exploratory extension to probe whether the gap generalizes beyond NER; RE conclusions are preliminary.
Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{The correlation-selection gap.}
    $\rho$ up to 0.45 coexists with selection $\Delta{\approx}0$ against 13--18~pp oracle headroom. Log-probability---the only per-sample signal---is too compressed within instances to discriminate (median range 0.041 nats, pooled within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){=}0.248$); instance-level signals, even when converted to per-sample consensus-proximity rankings (MBR), do not improve selection because utility correlates with F1 across instances but not within them. Single-metric evaluation risks selecting the wrong signal.

    \item \textbf{Diagnostic ablations.}
    A 4-seed free-form decoding ablation confirms NER compression is not caused by constrained decoding; entity-token LP increases within-instance variance ($4{\times}$) but worsens selection, pointing to intrinsic model overconfidence on entity tokens. Verbalized confidence is even more compressed, confirming compression is not LP-specific (Appendix~\ref{app:verbalized_confidence}).

    \item \textbf{Difficulty- and task-dependent signal rankings.}
    Rankings shift with task complexity and collapse at the capability boundary.\footnote{RE conclusions rest on SciERC alone (single benchmark, 40\% zero-F1 rate); we mark RE results with $^\dagger$RE throughout.}
\end{enumerate}

